The primary objective of this article is to present a project entitled Mobility Plan of the Cracow University of Technology, developed in alignment with the principles of mobility management. Initiated at the beginning of 2025 by the Department of Transportation Systems at the Cracow University of Technology, the project is scheduled for completion in 2026 with the publication of a strategic document bearing the same title.
[…] The project will be implemented in accordance with widely accepted methodology for mobility plans developed for traffic generators. In recent years, mobility planning in Poland has been increasingly aligned with preparation of SUMPs, which follow a rigorous and evolving methodological framework endorsed by the European Commission. The development team at the Cracow University of Technology will integrate these standards and best practices into the project, ensuring a contemporary and sustainable approach to mobility planning that:
(1). Prioritizes not only improved traffic flow, but also accessibility, quality of life, social inclusion, public health, environmental sustainability, and economic efficiency;
(2). Engages interdisciplinary planning teams composed of transport experts, urban planners, architects, and sociologists;
(3). Emphasizes inclusive, participatory planning by actively involving the local community in this case, the university's academic and administrative population throughout the planning process.
